<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    vibesupertonic-ui — the window you open to follow along and change knobs.

    Its own process, decided 2026-08-16 (docs/LINUX-PORT-PLAN.md, Phase 6
    conflict 2) and it is the Linux convention: PipeWire has pavucontrol,
    NetworkManager has nm-applet, CUPS has its applet. Three things depended on
    the answer and none was taste — the daemon must start headless, Xlib's error
    handler is process-global and Avalonia installs its own, and R-1 is enforced
    by a process boundary here instead of by discipline.

    So: this project may reference Core, and talks to the daemon over the same
    socket and the same verbs any external client uses. It has no privileged
    path, and adding the speech-dispatcher front-end later costs nothing because
    of it.

    Avalonia lives HERE and nowhere else — never in Core, and never in the
    daemon, which would pull a GUI toolkit into a process that has to run without
    a display. 11.3.20 is the same pin the two spikes carry.

    Not VstPortable: it links a GUI toolkit and is built for the Linux product.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>WinExe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <AssemblyName>vibesupertonic-ui</AssemblyName>

    <!--
      NO ICU, matching the daemon and vst-ctl — and this was the ONE binary that
      did not, which is a thing nobody would find on a desktop.

      Without it .NET probes for libicuuc/libicudata at process start and ABORTS
      if none is there, before Main runs. Measured 2026-08-27: the shipped UI
      probed for libicuuc.so.78, .79, .80 and .81 in turn. Every desktop has one,
      so it works everywhere a person would look — and it made
      this binary fail to start at all in a bare ubuntu:22.04, which is the
      first machine that ever ran it without a desktop already installed.

      That mattered because INSTALL.txt promises "No runtime to install. All
      three binaries carry what they need", and for this one that was false.
      Found by build/smoke-test.sh in CI, which is exactly the kind of
      finding about the archive that job exists to produce.

      What it costs: culture-aware collation and formatting become invariant.
      The UI sorts language names that are ASCII English and formats sizes and
      rates, so nothing here changes; text rendering is HarfBuzz and never
      touched ICU.
    -->
    <InvariantGlobalization>true</InvariantGlobalization>
    <RootNamespace>VibeSuperTonic.Ui</RootNamespace>
    <RuntimeIdentifiers>linux-x64</RuntimeIdentifiers>
    <VstPortable>false</VstPortable>
    <!-- <Version> comes from Directory.Build.props — every assembly, one number. -->
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia" Version="11.3.20" />
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia.Desktop" Version="11.3.20" />
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia.Themes.Fluent" Version="11.3.20" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\VibeSuperTonic.Core\VibeSuperTonic.Core.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
